WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.315
Lesson 12.4.

00:00:02.315 --> 00:00:04.510
Treasury and the liquidity ladder.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.854
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:06.854 --> 00:00:09.868
to structure reserves so money is available when it's

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:12.045
needed, earning a return at every tier.

00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:14.635
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.635 --> 00:00:16.320
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.035
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.035 --> 00:00:20.560
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.722
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.022 --> 00:00:26.016
Banks match the timing of assets to

00:00:26.016 --> 00:00:27.867
the timing of what they owe.

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:32.174
Your ladder: Refill downward on a schedule: T1 tops

00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:35.622
up T0 monthly; maturing PTs top up T1.

00:00:35.622 --> 00:00:38.063
Never fund a T0 need by selling

00:00:38.063 --> 00:00:40.652
a T3 position in a bad market.

00:00:40.652 --> 00:00:42.977
That's what the ladder exists to prevent.

00:00:42.977 --> 00:00:46.501
T0 Instant, Seconds, Stablecoins in the

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:49.797
operating wallet, 1 month: $5,000.

00:00:49.797 --> 00:00:54.196
T1 Same day, Hours, Blue-chip stablecoin lending,

00:00:54.196 --> 00:00:58.367
split across 2 protocols, 5 months: $25,000.

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:01.546
T2 Term, Scheduled, PTs maturing on dates

00:01:01.546 --> 00:01:04.725
you'll need the money (Lesson 10.1), Next

00:01:04.725 --> 00:01:07.447
6–12 months of planned spending.

00:01:07.447 --> 00:01:11.102
T3 Growth, Days–weeks, Strategy positions, LPs,

00:01:11.102 --> 00:01:13.463
staking, The rest, within caps.

00:01:14.763 --> 00:01:17.688
Let's look at the diagram: The liquidity ladder.

00:01:17.688 --> 00:01:19.248
Take a moment with it.

00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:21.658
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:01:22.958 --> 00:01:25.473
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:25.473 --> 00:01:28.655
T0 $5,000 + T1 $25,000

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:33.243
= 6 months instantly available.

00:01:33.243 --> 00:01:36.410
T2 holds three PTs maturing in 3, 6 and

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:40.353
9 months, each sized for 3 months of spending.

00:01:40.353 --> 00:01:43.313
A −50% market day touches none of it.

00:01:44.613 --> 00:01:46.073
Here's your checklist.

00:01:46.073 --> 00:01:47.608
Do it now, for real.

00:01:47.608 --> 00:01:51.983
First, T0 + T1 ≥ 6 months of obligations.

00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:55.108
Next, T2 maturities matched to planned spending.

00:01:55.108 --> 00:01:57.503
Then, Monthly refill schedule set.

00:01:57.503 --> 00:02:00.803
And finally, T3 never used for day-to-day needs.

00:02:02.103 --> 00:02:03.313
Question 1.

00:02:03.313 --> 00:02:06.648
Why hold T0 in plain stablecoins earning little?

00:02:11.068 --> 00:02:13.673
The answer: Instant, certain access.

00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:14.961
It's the tier that pays

00:02:14.961 --> 00:02:17.053
today's bills without selling anything.

00:02:18.353 --> 00:02:19.598
Question 2.

00:02:19.598 --> 00:02:20.813
What's T2 for?

00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:28.306
The answer: Known future spending, with maturities

00:02:28.306 --> 00:02:30.475
matched to when the money is needed

00:02:30.475 --> 00:02:32.223
and a fixed return locked in.

00:02:33.523 --> 00:02:34.793
Question 3.

00:02:34.793 --> 00:02:36.208
What does the ladder prevent?

00:02:40.628 --> 00:02:43.590
The answer: Forced selling of growth positions

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.047
at bad prices to meet short-term needs.

00:02:47.347 --> 00:02:49.862
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:49.862 --> 00:02:52.802
Next up, Lesson 12.5: Being the lender:

00:02:52.802 --> 00:02:56.037
supplying, curating and pricing credit risk.
